{"id":793,"date":"2020-11-05T11:38:47","date_gmt":"2020-11-05T19:38:47","guid":{"rendered":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/?post_type=lesson&#038;p=793"},"modified":"2023-08-21T08:41:34","modified_gmt":"2023-08-21T15:41:34","slug":"functions-2","status":"publish","type":"lesson","link":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/functions-2\/","title":{"rendered":"3C: Functions"},"content":{"rendered":"<div class=\"wpb-content-wrapper\"><p>[vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2216708c520\" name=\"6a2216708c520\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 1<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>What is a function?<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Functions are a way to save time and energy by storing commonly used lines of code into one named set. For example, to text a friend, you could write multiple lines of code to do all of this:<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">Take out phone\r\n\r\nUnlock phone\r\n\r\nOpen messaging app\r\n\r\nHave I texted this friend before?\r\n\r\nIf yes, go to last text message with friend\r\n\r\nIf no, compose new message and add friend\u2019s name\r\n\r\nWrite text message\r\n\r\nHit send<\/pre>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Or you could just use a function called \u00a0<code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">friend.text()<\/code>\u00a0<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">at the beginning of every new program!<\/span>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2216708c67d\" name=\"6a2216708c67d\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 2<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Defining and calling functions<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">The first step to writing your own function is the term <\/span><b>def<\/b><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">. You are telling Python that you are \u201cdefining a function\u201d.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Next comes your function name followed by parentheses and a colon. Your function name can be multiple words, but you need to add an underscore between every word.\u00a0<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">In this step, you are going to create your own function to have your CoDrone Mini fly in a square! Your function name will look like this:<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">def square():<\/pre>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Add a new indented line for the body of the function, and then write your square code below. You can use one of your old square programs if you saved it!<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">To run your function, write the function name underneath the function\u2019s definition. If you don&#8217;t call the function, it won&#8217;t run!<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">def square():\r\n\r\n    drone.takeoff()\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 for i in range(1):\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0    drone.set_yaw(0)\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.set_pitch(30)\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.move(1)\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.set_pitch(0)\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.set_yaw(20)\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.move(2)\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.land()\r\n\r\nsquare()<\/pre>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Import the CoDrone Mini and time libraries, create the drone object, and pair. Next, try running your code now. What happened?<\/span>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2216708c715\" name=\"6a2216708c715\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 3<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Parameters in functions<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">After every function are parentheses, and this is where the parameters go. Parameters are variables the function needs to use, but not all functions will have parameters! In the previous example, you might have noticed that the function had an empty set of parentheses.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Think of a function as the processes part of a large factory. Some processes can run as-is. However, some processes need more information to run, like a program that will greet you personally by name!<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">For this activity, go back to the square program you just made and add two parameters in the parentheses: power and duration.<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">def square(power, duration)<\/pre>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Then you can change some of the parameters in your program:<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">def square(power, duration):\r\n\r\n    drone.takeoff()\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 for i in range(1):\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0    drone.set_yaw(0)\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.set_pitch(power)\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.move(duration)\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.set_pitch(0)\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.set_yaw(20)\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.move(2)\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.land()\r\n\r\nsquare()<\/pre>\n<p>The power and time will be set when the function is called. For example, if you want the power to be set at 30 and the time for each command to be 1 second, then you would call your function in your main program like this:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">square(30, 1)<\/pre>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Try running your program now. Did you notice anything different from when you had no parameters in your function?<\/span>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2216708c7c3\" name=\"6a2216708c7c3\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 4<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Return functions<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">A return function is slightly different from the functions you just saw. A return function returns a value or set of values. The values that are returned are called <\/span><b>return values<\/b><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">. Very creative, we know.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">One real-life example of a return function is a calculator. You input an equation, and once you hit the equal sign, the calculator returns an answer! In this activity, your CoDrone Mini will simulate a calculator. After adding two numbers in the return function together, it will throttle up and down for the value of the answer.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">After importing the CoDrone Mini and time libraries, creating the drone object, and pairing, define a function called \u00a0<code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">add<\/code>\u00a0<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">\u00a0with <\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">x<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\"> and<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\"> y<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\"> as parameters. Underneath, have the function return \u00a0<code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">x+y<\/code>\u00a0<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">.<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">import CoDrone_mini\r\n\r\nimport time\r\n\r\ndrone = CoDrone_mini.CoDrone()\r\n\r\ndrone.pair()\r\n\r\n\r\ndef add(x,y):\r\n\r\n    return x+y<\/pre>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Create a variable called<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\"> \u00a0<code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">answer<\/code>\u00a0<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">that will store the return function. Next, include a print command that will display <\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">\u201cThe answer is\u201d<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\"> along with the answer.\u00a0<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">answer = add(2, 3)\r\n\r\nprint(\u201cThe answer is \u201c, answer)<\/pre>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Program your drone to take off, and then create a for loop with <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">answer<\/code><\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">\u00a0as its parameter. Within the loop, have the drone throttle up for half a second at 30% power, and then down for the same amount of time and power. Finally, have the drone land.<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">drone.takeoff()\r\n\r\nfor i in range(answer):\r\n\r\n    drone.set_throttle(30)\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.move(0.5)\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.set_throttle(-30)\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.move(0.5)\r\n \r\ndrone.land()<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2216708c82e\" name=\"6a2216708c82e\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 5<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Final code<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Your program should look like this:<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">import CoDrone_mini\r\n\r\nimport time\r\n\r\ndrone = CoDrone_mini.CoDrone()\r\n\r\ndrone.pair()\r\n\r\n\r\ndef add(x,y):\r\n\r\n    return x+y\r\n\r\n\r\nanswer = add(2, 3)\r\n\r\nprint(\u201cThe answer is \u201c, answer)\r\n\r\ndrone.takeoff()\r\n\r\nfor i in range(answer):\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.set_throttle(30)\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.move(0.5)\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.set_throttle(-30)\r\n\r\n    drone.move(0.5)\r\n\r\ndrone.land()<\/pre>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Try this out with multiple numbers! What happens when your drone gets to bigger numbers?<\/span>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2216708c8ec\" name=\"6a2216708c8ec\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 6<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Return functions with user input<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">In this step, you\u2019re going to take the calculator simulation even further by having a user input the numbers to add together!<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Keep the first portion of your program the same:<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">import CoDrone_mini\r\n\r\nimport time\r\n\r\ndrone = CoDrone_mini.CoDrone()\r\n\r\ndrone.pair()\r\n\r\n\r\ndef add(x,y):\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0return x+y<\/pre>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Set up a while loop so that while true, the program will run. Inside the loop, create a variable called \u00a0<code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">num1<\/code>\u00a0<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">and set its value to \u00a0<code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">input()<\/code>\u00a0<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">. In the input parameters, write <\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">&#8220;Input first number (or q to quit): &#8220;. <\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Next, write an if statement so that if <\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">\u201cq\u201d<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\"> is input, the program will break out of the loop. Create another variable called <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">num2<\/code><\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">\u00a0<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">and set its value to <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">input()<\/code><\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">\u00a0<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">as well. Write <\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">&#8220;Input second number: &#8221; <\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">in the parameters.<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">while True:\r\n\r\n    num1 = input(\"Input first number (or q to quit): \")\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 if num1 == \"q\":\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0    break\r\n\r\n    num2 = input(\"Input second number: \")\r\n<\/pre>\n<p>While still in the while loop, create a variable called \u00a0<code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">answer<\/code>\u00a0that will use the \u00a0<code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">add(x,y)<\/code>\u00a0function to return the sum of <span style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">num1<\/code><\/span> and <span style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">num2<\/code><\/span>. Next, include a print command that will print the answer.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">answer = add(int(num1), int(num2))\r\n\r\n    print(\"The answer is \", answer)<\/pre>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">The rest of your program will be the same!<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">drone.takeoff()\r\n\r\n    for i in range(answer):\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0    drone.set_throttle(30)\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0  drone.move(0.5)\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0  drone.set_throttle(-30)\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0  drone.move(0.5)\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.land()<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2216708c951\" name=\"6a2216708c951\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 7<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Final code<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Your final program should look like this:<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">import CoDrone_mini\r\n\r\nimport time\r\n\r\ndrone = CoDrone_mini.CoDrone()\r\n\r\ndrone.pair()\r\n\r\n\r\ndef add(x,y):\r\n\r\n    return x+y\r\n\r\n\r\nwhile True:\r\n\r\n    num1 = input(\"Input first number (or q to quit): \")\r\n\r\n    if num1 == \"q\":\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0    break\r\n\r\n    num2 = input(\"Input second number: \")\r\n\r\n\u00a0\r\n    answer = add(int(num1), int(num2))\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 print(\"The answer is \", answer)\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.takeoff()\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 for i in range(answer):\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0    drone.set_throttle(30)\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0  drone.move(0.5)\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0  drone.set_throttle(-30)\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0  drone.move(0.5)\r\n\r\n    drone.land()<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2216708c9ca\" name=\"6a2216708c9ca\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 8<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Challenge<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<b>Challenge: Please Excuse My Dear Aunt Sally<\/b><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">You know how to add, but now try making functions for subtraction, multiplication, and division! Once you have that figured out, try adding parentheses and brackets to see if your CoDrone Mini can use the order of operations to solve your equation.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Hints:<\/span><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Subtraction sign: &#8211;<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Multiplication sign: *<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Division sign: \/<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Exponent sign: ^<\/span><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row]<\/p>\n<\/div>","protected":false},"excerpt":{"rendered":"[vc_row][vc_column] Step 9 What is a function? [vc_column_text]Functions are a way to save time and energy by storing commonly used lines of code into one named set. For example, to&nbsp;<a href=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/functions-2\/\">&hellip;<\/a>","protected":false},"featured_media":1730,"template":"","meta":{"_initial_content":"","_new_post":false,"_quiz_has_questions":false,"_lesson_complexity":"","_lesson_length":10,"_lesson_course":744,"_lesson_preview":"preview"},"lesson-category":[1782,1848,1793,1826,1482,853,694,171,63],"lesson-tag":[2135,2125,2131,2122,2134],"class_list":["post-793","lesson","type-lesson","status-publish","has-post-thumbnail","hentry","lesson-tag-beginner","lesson-tag-codrone-mini","lesson-tag-functions","lesson-tag-programming","lesson-tag-python","module-beginner","post","odd"],"_links":{"self":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/793","targetHints":{"allow":["GET"]}}],"collection":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons"}],"about":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/types\/lesson"}],"version-history":[{"count":4,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/793\/revisions"}],"predecessor-version":[{"id":2869,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/793\/revisions\/2869"}],"wp:featuredmedia":[{"embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media\/1730"}],"wp:attachment":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media?parent=793"}],"wp:term":[{"taxonomy":"lesson-category","embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lesson-category?post=793"},{"taxonomy":"lesson-tag","embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lesson-tag?post=793"}],"curies":[{"name":"wp","href":"https:\/\/api.w.org\/{rel}","templated":true}]}}